--TEST--
The description is asked of the result set and never of a row, so `Core\Db\Row` has no `columns` to answer with the row's own labels — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $rows = $db->query("select id, owner from accounts", []);

// The two readings a result set carries, side by side: the rows are what the
// statement answered and the columns are what it described, and the second is
// readable whether or not there is a single one of the first.
foreach ($rows->columns() as Core\Db\Column $column) {
    string $name = $column->name();
    Core\Db\ColumnType $type = $column->type();
    bool $nullable = $column->nullable();
}

foreach ($rows->all() as Core\Db\Row $row) {
    // A row reads values by the label the description carries — the same
    // labels, asked of the other half.
    mixed $value = $row->get("owner");
    bool $present = $row->has("owner");
}

// And the refusal that says which half owns the description: a row is a
// string-keyed reading of one row's values, so asking it what the statement
// described is a call with no member behind it rather than a second, weaker
// answer to the question `Rows::columns` already answers.
foreach ($rows->all() as Core\Db\Row $named) {
    mixed $perRow = $named->columns();
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Core\Db\Row` has no method named `columns`
   --> case.nvs:26:21
